// Pixel / sample OUTPUT — the inverse of `media`, and the sibling of `speech`.
// See design.md D11.
//
// Part of the `pie:inferlet` package. The vendored copies live under the
// builds' `deps/` trees (kept in sync via `scripts/sync-wit.sh` / `wit-deps`);
// this file is the source of truth.
//
// THE POINT OF THIS INTERFACE IS THAT PIXELS DO NOT CROSS INTO WASM. A decode
// stage hands the guest a `frames` handle the way `media.image` hands it a
// preprocessed span: the payload stays host-side (host memory today, a device
// pointer once the VAE writes one), and the guest holds a handle plus the
// facts it needs to size a job — width, height, count, fps.
//
// Two doors lead out of a handle, and they are not the same door:
//
//   * `encode(format)` returns the encoded bytes AS A GUEST VALUE. That is a
//     copy into linear memory, taken deliberately, for a guest that wants to
//     hash the file, splice it into a multipart body, or hand it to
//     `wasi:filesystem`. A 4K clip through this door is a 4K clip in the
//     guest's heap.
//   * `session.send-frames` encodes and streams to the client WITHOUT the
//     bytes ever being materialized in linear memory. This is the door the
//     happy path uses, and the reason the resource exists rather than a
//     `list<u8>`-returning function.
//
// `from-rgb8` is the host-side constructor: a guest (or a test) that has raw
// interleaved RGB8 can build a handle before any VAE exists.
//
// `from-channel` is the OTHER constructor, and the one the VAE road uses. A
// VAE decode pass lands its pixels on the `pixels` seam; the pass's eta
// epilogue reads that seam with `intrinsics::pixels()` and puts it on one of
// its own channels. `from-channel` takes that channel's
// committed cell HOST-SIDE — the same take `channel.take` would do, minus the
// lift into linear memory — and builds the handle from it. The pixels go
// engine -> host -> encoder -> client and never enter WASM.
//
// WHY THIS AND NOT A VERB ON THE PASS. A `forward-pass.frames()` reading the
// fire's `LaneReadout { seam: pixels }` would be one call shorter, but it puts
// the pass's fire bookkeeping on the output path: the handle would have to
// name WHICH fire (a pipeline runs several in flight), the pass would have to
// retain a readout the guest may never ask for, and a guest that wants to
// touch the pixels at all — a tile blend, a crop, a contact sheet of four
// decodes — would have no door but a second, different one. The channel is
// already the pass's answer surface, already carries the fire's ordering and
// its poison, and already lets a guest choose between looking (`take`) and not
// looking (`from-channel`). So the seam a guest reads a decode back through is
// the seam it hands to the encoder, and the only thing `from-channel` adds is
// the promise that the bytes never cross.
interface frames {

    use types.{error, blob};
    use channel.{channel};

    // How a `frames` handle is serialized.
    //
    // `png`, `jpeg` and `webp` are STILLS: they are valid only when
    // `count() == 1`, and a multi-frame handle refuses them by name rather
    // than silently encoding frame 0. `webp` is the lossless VP8L encoder
    // (pure Rust); it is not the animated form.
    //
    // `raw-rgb8` is the parity format — exactly the bytes the handle holds,
    // `count * height * width * 3`, no header — so a test can compare against
    // what it put in. `y4m` is the parity format for a clip: an uncompressed
    // sequence any decoder reads, which is what makes an mp4 regression
    // provable rather than merely visible.
    //
    // `mp4-h264` is the real video format: H.264 High profile from NVENC,
    // muxed into ISO base media (avc1 + avcC). It needs an NVIDIA encoder on
    // the machine and a runtime built with the CUDA shell; without either it
    // refuses by name, saying which half is missing.
    enum image-format {
        png,
        jpeg,
        webp,
        raw-rgb8,
        y4m,
        mp4-h264,
    }

    // How a `pcm` handle is serialized. `wav` is 16-bit PCM RIFF (the form
    // every player takes); `raw-f32` is the parity format — the samples as
    // little-endian f32, interleaved, no header.
    enum audio-format {
        wav,
        raw-f32,
    }

    // A host-side handle to decoded pixels: `count` frames of `width` x
    // `height` interleaved RGB8, in presentation order.
    //
    // Self-describing, like `speech`: the guest never hardcodes a model's
    // latent geometry or frame rate, it asks. A still image is a handle with
    // `count() == 1`; `fps()` is then meaningless and reads 0.
    resource frames {

        // Build a handle from raw interleaved RGB8. `bytes` must be exactly
        // `count * height * width * 3` long; every other length is refused by
        // name with both figures.
        //
        // This is the constructor that lets a test, or a guest holding pixels
        // it made itself, reach the encoders before a VAE decode pass
        // exists to produce a handle the other way.
        from-rgb8: static func(bytes: blob,
                               width: u32,
                               height: u32,
                               count: u32,
                               fps: f32) -> result<frames, error>;

        // Build a handle from a channel's committed cell, WITHOUT the pixels
        // entering linear memory. This is the VAE road: a
        // VAE decode pass's epilogue reads `intrinsics::pixels()` and puts
        // the plane on `ch`; this takes that cell host-side, the way
        // `channel.take` would, and turns it into frames.
        //
        // The cell must be `count * height * width * 3` f32 in the model's own
        // range, [-1, 1] — what the `pixels` seam lands for an RGB decoder —
        // one row per output voxel in `(t, h, w)` order, `w` fastest, which is
        // exactly presentation order. Values are mapped to RGB8 by
        // `(x + 1) / 2` and CLAMPED, so a decoder that overshoots produces a
        // saturated pixel rather than an error. Any other length, or a channel
        // that is not f32, is refused by name with both figures.
        //
        // The cell is CONSUMED, like `take`: the same channel feeds the next
        // fire. It blocks on an empty cell under the same discipline as
        // `channel.take-blocking` — the guest's task waits for the fire that
        // fills it, and a poisoned channel surfaces that fire's failure.
        from-channel: static func(ch: borrow<channel>,
                                  width: u32,
                                  height: u32,
                                  count: u32,
                                  fps: f32) -> result<frames, error>;

        // **THE WAY IN**: decode an encoded still (PNG / JPEG / GIF / WebP)
        // into a handle. The format is SNIFFED from the bytes — a caller
        // holding a file holds its magic too, and a stated format that
        // disagreed with the bytes would be one more thing to get wrong.
        //
        // This is `encode`'s pair, and it is what lets a guest hand a
        // PICTURE to a VAE encode pass: img2img, inpainting, a
        // reference lane. `media.image` is the other door and the wrong one
        // here — it patchifies for a vision tower and answers TOKENS, and a
        // diffusion model has no vision front-end for it to ask.
        //
        // Errors, by name and with the byte count, when the bytes decode to
        // nothing. A picture that did not decode is not a black picture.
        decode: static func(bytes: blob) -> result<frames, error>;

        // Join `parts`, in order, into one handle host-side: a clip decoded
        // a fire at a time is the concatenation of each fire's frames. Every
        // part must have the same width, height and fps; a mismatch, or no
        // parts at all, is refused by name with both figures.
        concat: static func(parts: list<borrow<frames>>) -> result<frames, error>;

        // **THE VAE ROAD, RUN BACKWARDS**: put these pixels into `ch`'s
        // cell, the inverse of `from-channel` and to the same statute — the
        // bytes do not enter linear memory.
        //
        // The cell is written as `count * height * width * 3` f32 in the
        // model's own range, [-1, 1], one row per voxel in `(t, h, w)`
        // order with `w` fastest, which is what a VAE encode pass's
        // pixel input reads. RGB8 maps by `2 * (x / 255) - 1`, exactly
        // undoing `from-channel`'s `(x + 1) / 2`.
        //
        // The put is `channel.put`'s — the SEED a channel takes before its
        // first fire, not `set`'s rewrite of a cell already in the ring
        // (a channel has no ring until a fire has run). So this is a
        // channel a pass binds as an INPUT, seeded exactly once, and not
        // one an epilogue writes. A channel whose declared shape is not
        // this many f32 is refused by name with both figures.
        to-channel: func(ch: borrow<channel>) -> result<_, error>;

        // Frame width in pixels.
        width: func() -> u32;

        // Frame height in pixels.
        height: func() -> u32;

        // Number of frames. 1 for a still.
        count: func() -> u32;

        // Frames per second. 0 for a still, and for a clip whose producer
        // stated no rate.
        fps: func() -> f32;

        // Encode into `format` and return the bytes AS A GUEST VALUE — a copy
        // into linear memory. Prefer `session.send-frames` when the bytes are
        // headed for the client and nothing in the guest reads them.
        //
        // Errors: a still format against a multi-frame handle, `mp4-h264`
        // without an NVIDIA encoder, or an encoder failure — each by name.
        encode: func(format: image-format) -> result<blob, error>;
    }

    // A host-side handle to decoded audio: interleaved f32 samples in
    // [-1, 1] at `rate` Hz. The audio half of the same statute — the samples
    // stay host-side and `session.send-pcm` streams the encoded file out
    // without them entering linear memory.
    resource pcm {

        // Build a handle from interleaved f32 samples. `channels` must divide
        // the sample count.
        from-f32: static func(samples: list<f32>,
                              rate: u32,
                              channels: u32) -> result<pcm, error>;

        // Sample rate in Hz.
        rate: func() -> u32;

        // Channel count (interleaved).
        channels: func() -> u32;

        // Encode into `format` and return the bytes as a guest value. Total,
        // because both formats are a header plus a transcode of samples the
        // handle already holds: there is nothing here that can fail.
        encode: func(format: audio-format) -> blob;
    }
}
